<Project>
  <!-- MSBuild applies only the CLOSEST Directory.Build.props, so the repository root's settings
       have to be imported explicitly. Without this line every shipped project silently loses the
       root's TargetFramework, nullable settings, package metadata and AutoVersioning reference,
       and nothing says so, because the defaults it falls back to still compile. Same idiom as
       tests/Directory.Build.props. -->
  <Import Project="$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove('Directory.Build.props', '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../'))" />

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Every shipped assembly declares itself trimmable.

      ⛔ This travels INSIDE the package. IsTrimmable is compiled into the assembly as
      [AssemblyMetadata("IsTrimmable", "True")], and an app publishing with TrimMode=partial trims
      ONLY assemblies carrying that mark. Leave it off and a consumer's trimmed publish keeps these
      assemblies whole and outside its trim analysis. Nothing fails; the publish is just larger,
      and silent about it.

      ⚠ Learned the expensive way: the first releases of the AppServices and ScopedEditors packages
      shipped without it. Their source repository set it in a nested src/Directory.Build.props that
      a file-by-file move never read, and the bbpkg template they were generated from did not set
      it at all. It does now, which is why every repository generated since has this file. The code itself
      measured clean both times; only the declaration was missing, which is exactly the kind of
      loss that no build and no test notices on its own. PackageMetadataTests now reads the mark
      off the COMPILED assembly so it cannot go missing quietly again.
    -->
    <IsTrimmable>true</IsTrimmable>

    <!--
      Run the Roslyn trim analyser at THIS repository's build, so a trim break reddens a plain
      `dotnet build -c Release` here rather than surfacing later in somebody else's app publish,
      attributed to an assembly that app cannot edit.

      ⛔ A packaged library is outside every consuming app's build graph, so nothing downstream
      will ever run the analyser on this code again. If it does not run here, it does not run.

      ⚠ The analyser cannot see compiled XAML. XamlIl weaves that IL into the assembly after Roslyn
      has finished, so a library with .axaml files needs an ILLink pass as well: a trimmed publish
      of a consumer that roots each assembly with TrimmerRootAssembly and sets
      TrimmerSingleWarn=false.

      ⛔ IsAotCompatible is deliberately NOT set. It would also switch on the AOT and single-file
      analysers, which is a claim about these libraries that nothing has measured.

      Scoped to src/ deliberately: tests/ has its own Directory.Build.props, is never published or
      trimmed, and reflects over assemblies on purpose, so analysing it would be noise.
    -->
    <EnableTrimAnalyzer>true</EnableTrimAnalyzer>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
